Linux as an Operating System
Linux acts as a bridge between computer hardware and application software.
It manages hardware resources and provides services to applications.
System Information – uname
The uname command provides basic information about the Linux system.
It displays details such as the kernel name, kernel version, and system architecture.
CPU Information – lscpu
The lscpu command displays information about the processor.
It shows the CPU architecture, number of CPUs, cores, and threads.
Linux uses this information to manage and schedule processes.
Storage Management – lsblk
The lsblk command displays connected storage devices.
It provides details such as disk names, partitions, sizes, and mount points.
Linux manages these devices using block devices and filesystems.
Process Management – ps
The ps command displays currently running processes.
It provides information such as PID, CPU usage, memory usage, and process state.
This demonstrates how Linux manages multiple processes.
Real-Time Monitoring – top
The top command continuously monitors system activity.
It displays CPU utilization, memory usage, system load, and active processes.
It helps users understand how system resources are being utilized.
Memory Abstraction
Linux provides virtual memory to applications.
Programs do not need to directly control or manage physical RAM.
The kernel handles memory allocation and management.
Storage Abstraction
Applications access storage through files and directories.
They do not need to directly access physical disk sectors.
This makes storage management easier and safer.
Device Management
Linux manages input and output devices through device drivers.
Examples include keyboards, USB devices, displays, and network devices.
Device drivers allow applications to communicate with hardware.
Role of the Linux Kernel
The kernel is responsible for managing important system resources.
It controls CPU, memory, storage, processes, and I/O devices.
It provides a standard interface between applications and hardware.
Importance of Linux Commands
uname → Provides system and kernel information.
lscpu → Provides CPU and processor details.
lsblk → Shows storage devices and partitions.
ps → Displays running processes.
top → Provides real-time resource monitoring.
Overall Function
Linux hides the complexity of hardware from applications.
It allows multiple programs to share resources efficiently.
It provides security, resource management, and convenient access to hardware.
Conclusion
Linux works as both a resource manager and an abstraction layer.
It connects application software with computer hardware.
Commands such as uname, lscpu, lsblk, ps, and top help users observe how Linux manages the system.
